Patch summary:
Pace the Cuttlefish AIDL audio streams on ALSA alone, and recover ALSA
streams that stall.

The primary HAL's emulator workaround (b/302587331) sleeps to wall clock and
skips a whole buffer whenever the stream falls behind. On input, that skips
the real ALSA read and returns zero-filled buffers to AudioRecord. On output,
it drops an audible 85 ms chunk of playback. virtio-snd playback is paced in
real time by crosvm, so remove the workaround.

StreamAlsa also enables MonoPipe's blocking-writer throttle for input even
though ALSA capture reads are already hardware paced. The extra delay makes
the worker progressively fall behind until virtio-snd returns EIO. Conversely,
the client-facing transfers never wait: input substitutes silence whenever the
worker has not filled the pipe yet, and output drops the whole buffer whenever
the worker has not emptied the pipe yet.

Remove MonoPipe's second capture throttle. The capture worker never waits for
the client: it drops what does not fit into the pipe, so ALSA keeps draining.
Capture gets an ALSA buffer and pipe twice the stream buffer, since the worker
reads a whole stream buffer at a time and an equal-sized ALSA buffer overruns
on any delay. Client transfers wait for the workers, for at most two buffer
durations, instead of substituting silence or dropping a buffer.

A virtio-snd stream can stall with no I/O in flight: when a full buffer of
I/O completes between two pointer updates, the indirect pointer sees no
progress, so every transfer times out with EIO and nothing restarts it.
tinyalsa reports these failures as -1, so the EIO retries in alsa_utils never
apply. Stop the PCM after a failed read or write so the next transfer prepares
and restarts it. Without this, a stalled capture stream left the client read
blocked, the stream never reached standby, and microphone and playback stayed
silent until reboot.

